    "durationMs": 198153,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully tested. Built with ASP.NET Core 8 minimal APIs plus EF Core 8 + Npgsql (the idiomatic .NET persistence layer), pinned to 8.0.11 packages since Npgsql 8.0.15 doesn't exist on NuGet.\n\n**How it's wired:**\n- `DATABASE_URL` (PostgreSQL URL) is parsed into an Npgsql connection string at startup — nothing hard-coded\n- EF Core migration `InitialCreate` is scaffolded (`Migrations/`), with a unique index on `Code`; pending migrations apply at startup via `Database.Migrate()` before the app serves\n- Click increments use `ExecuteUpdateAsync` (`clicks + 1` in SQL), so each redirect increments exactly once, atomically\n- `sh start.sh` builds (no-op if built, no installs), then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`\n\n**Verified against the requirements:**\n- POST `/links` → 201 `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}` with URL echoed byte-for-byte unchanged (tested with case-mixed host, port, query, fragment); auto-generated 8-char codes, zero collisions across 20 posts\n- Duplicate custom code → 409, malformed JSON / missing or invalid url / invalid code (length + charset boundaries 4 and 20) → 422 with `error` field\n- GET `/links/{code}` → 200 or 404 with error; GET `/r/{code}` → 302 with exact Location, clicks incremented once per request; GET `/links` → ascending creation order with `total`\n- Killed and restarted the server: data and click counts survived (3 → 4 across the restart)\n\nREADME includes the start instructions; test server is stopped and port 8000 is free."
  }
